[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 In computability theory, an *oracle* is a black-box that answ
ers questions about a designated set. A fundamental observation is that computa
bly enumerable (c.e.) sets are "cooperative" with search: the longer one runs a
n enumeration, the more elements one finds. We call such objects **attractors**
  they become easier to find the more effort is invested.[] 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1220.45793pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 60588--60588
[]\T1/lmtt/m/n/12 But is there a dual phenomenon? Can we identify objects that 
become *harder* to find as search effort increases? We call such objects **repu
lsors** or **avoiders**. At first glance, the existence of repulsors seems para
doxical: how can looking harder make finding harder?[] 
 []

